    "description": "When a $4 snack bag quietly drops from 200 g to 160 g, you don't pay 20% more\u2014you actually pay 25% more per gram. Here is the denominator math behind shrinkflation and why checking the tiny unit-price label on the shelf saves you money every trip.\n\nIllustrative unbranded example: $4 \u00f7 200 g = 2.0\u00a2/g vs. $4 \u00f7 160 g = 2.5\u00a2/g (+25% unit price increase).\n\nChapters:\n00:00 \u2014 20% Smaller. 25% Pricier.\n00:04 \u2014 Start With Unit Price\n00:10 \u2014 Same Price, Smaller Bag\n00:15 \u2014 20% Less Food Inside\n00:21 \u2014 Do the New Math\n00:26 \u2014 +25% More Per Gram\n00:32 \u2014 The Denominator Trap\n00:37 \u2014 Shrinkflation (BLS)\n00:43 \u2014 Always Check Unit Price\n\nSources:\n- U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics \u2014 Measuring shrinkflation and its impact on inflation: https://www.bls.gov/opub/btn/volume-12/measuring-shrinkflation-and-its-impact-on-inflation.htm\n\nHave you noticed shrinkflation on your favorite snack lately? Drop it in the comments!\n\n---\nGraphics created with AI assistance. All facts sourced from official documentation.\n\n#Shorts #Shrinkflation #EverydayEconomics #MoneyMath #PaperTrail #Explained",
